          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Goodwillie calculus is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential calculus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            are replaced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+functors"} topological functors
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
          %h3#as_an_approximation_to_stabilization_of_a_functor As an approximation to stabilization of a functor
          %p
            The operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            that sends an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Stab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stab(C)
            does not in general extend to a functor.
          %p
            We may think of this operation as the analog of
            %em linearizing
            a space. Turning an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C \to D
            into a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Stab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Stab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stab(C) \to Stab(D)
            is not unlike performing a first order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+expansion"} Taylor expansion
            of a function, whence one speaks here of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie-Taylor+towers"} Goodwillie-Taylor towers
          %p This is what Goodwillie calculus studies.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            are each either
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Top
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top_*
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            a sequence of spectra, called the derivatives of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mn 1
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mn 2
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi n
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_1 F, \partial_2 F,\cdots, \partial_n F, \cdots
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo *
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_* F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            the spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_n F
            has a natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo •
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_\bullet F
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+sequence"} symmetric sequence
            of spectra.
          %p
            The Goodwillie-derivatives of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            contain substantial information about the homotopy type of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            together with natural transformations forming a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goodwillie-Taylor+tower"} Goodwillie-Taylor tower
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mn 0
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F \to \cdots \to P_n F \to P_{n-1} F \to \cdots\to P_0 F
          %p
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_n F
            being the universal
            %em n-excisive
            approximation to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-excisive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} n-excisive
            if it takes any
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n + 1
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} analytic (∞,1)-functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %munder
                    %mi holim
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F(X) \simeq \underset{n}{holim} P_n F(X).
          %p
            The fibre
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_n F
            of the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_n F \to P_{n-1} F
            is an
            %em n-homogeneous
            functor in an appropriate sense, and is determined by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi n
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_n F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            takes values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec
            then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D_n F(X) \simeq (\partial_n F \wedge X^{\wedge n})_{h \Sigma_n}.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            takes values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Top
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top_*
            then one needs to prefix the right hand side with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{\infty}
          %h3#Analogy Analogy between homotopy theory and calculus
          %p
            Here is an overview of the relation between
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            theory and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+approaches+to+differential+calculus"} differential calculus
            that is the starting point for Goodwillie calculus.
          %blockquote
            %p
              based on a message by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
              to the Category Theory Mailing list, May 12, 2010
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in one variable over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
            bears some resemblances with the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            and the sum is the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+sum"} wedge sum
          %p The following dictionary indicates what the correspondence between the two subjects is.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo ↦
                        %mtext corresponds to
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \stackrel{\text{corresponds to}}{\mapsto}
                the category of
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+set"} pointed set
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the category of pointed
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the pointed circle;
            %li
              %p
                the augmentation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k[ [x] ] \to k)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} connected components functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
                \: pointed homotopy types
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                pointed sets
            %li
              %p
                the augmentation ideal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
                of pointed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected"} connected
                spaces;
            %li
              %p
                the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
                power of the augmentation ideal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^{n+1}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the subcategory of pointed
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected"} connected
                spaces;
            %li
              %p
                the product of an element in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^{n+1}
                with an element of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^{m+1}
                is an element of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mi m
                          %mo +
                          %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^{n+m+2}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
                of an
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected"} connected
                space with a
                = succeed "-connected" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
                = succeed "-connected;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mi m
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+m+1)
            %li
              %p
                multiplication by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
                functor.
            %li
              %p
                division by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
                functor;
              %p
                Notice here the difference: the loop functor is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                to the suspension functor, not its
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} group
            %li
              %p
                the ideal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J=x k[ [x] ]
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
                via division by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                similarly, the category of pointed connected spaces is equivalent to the category of topological groups via the loop space functor (it is actually an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
                of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            %li
              %p
                More generally, the ideal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^{n+1}
                is isomorphic to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
                via division by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{n+1}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                similarly, the category of
                = succeed "-connected" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
                of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %li
              %p
                the quotient ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]/J^{n+1}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
                the category of
                = succeed "-truncated" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "s)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy n-type
            %li
              %p
                The sequence of approximations of a formal power series
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi ⋯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)=a_0+a_1x+ \cdots
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0+a_1 x
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0+a_1 x + a_2 x^2
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+tower"} Postnikov tower
                of a pointed homotopy type
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\pi_0(X)]
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\pi_0(X); \pi_1(X)]
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\pi_0(X); \pi_1(X); \pi_2(X)]
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots
              %p
                Here,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X)
                is the set of connected components of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\pi_0(X); \pi_1(X)]
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\pi_0(X); \pi_1(X); \pi_2(X)]
                is the fundamental 2-groupoid of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                The differences between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
                and its successives approximations
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi R
                                %mn 0
                              %mo =
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo −
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 1
                              %mi x
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 2
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 3
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 3
                              %mo +
                              %mi ⋯
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi R
                                %mn 1
                              %mo =
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo −
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 0
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 1
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 2
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 3
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 3
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 4
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 4
                              %mo +
                              %mi ⋯
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi R
                                %mn 2
                              %mo =
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo −
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 0
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 1
                              %mi x
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 3
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 3
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 4
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 4
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi a
                                %mn 5
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 5
                              %mo +
                              %mi ⋯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{aligned}
                      R_0 = f(x)-a_0  &amp;= a_1 x+a_2 x^2+a_3 x^3+ \cdots
                      \\\
                      R_1 = f(x)-(a_0+a_1 x) &amp;= a_2 x^2 + a_3 x^3 + a_4 x^4+ \cdots
                      \\\
                      R_2 = f(x)-(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2) &amp;= a_3 x^3 + a_4 x^4 + a_5 x^5 +\cdots
                      \\end{aligned}
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0=[0;\pi_1(X), \pi_2(X), \pi_3(X), \cdots]
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1=[0;0, \pi_2(X), \pi_3(X), \cdots]
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2=[0;0, 0, \pi_3(X), \cdots]
              %p
                Here,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
                is the connected component of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                at the base point,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                constructed by from paths starting at the base point,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2
                is the universal 2-cover of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                constructed from paths starting the base point, etc.
            %li
              %p
                Division by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is shifting down the coefficients of a power series.
              %p
                If
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 2
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %mi ⋯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)=a_1 x+a_2 x^2 + \cdots
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi ⋯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)/x= a_1+a_2 x+ \cdots
              %p Similarly, the loop space functor is shifting down the homotopy groups of a pointed space:
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=[a_0,a_1,a_2,...]
                then
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)=[a_1,a_2,....]
              %p
                Unfortunately, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
                functor does not shift up the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
                homotopy groups of an
                = succeed "-connected" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n \geq 1)
                by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freudenthal+suspension+theorem"} Freudenthal suspension theorem
              %p
                For example, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=[0;0,a_2, a_3,...]
                then
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi X
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ;
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 3
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma X=[0;0,0,a_2,a_3...]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 4
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 5
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=[0;0,0, a_3, a_4, a_5,...]
                then
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi X
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ;
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 3
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 4
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 5
                        %mo ,
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo .
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma X=[0;0,0, 0, a_3, a_4, a_5,...]
              %p
                In other words, the canonical map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \Omega X
                is a
                = succeed "-equivalence" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected"} connected
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n \geq 1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X[2n]
                denotes the
                = succeed "-type" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                (the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} truncation
                of
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X[2n]  \to \Omega \Sigma X[2n] \simeq \Omega \Sigma X[2n+1]
                \.
          %p …
          %p
            From a blog
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/12/smooth_structures_in_ottawa.html#c020698"} discussion
          %p
            %a{:href => "#AroneKankaanrinta95"} Arone Kankaanrinta 95
            write
          %blockquote
            %p The Goodwillie tower of the identity…is a tower of functors and natural transformations, which starts with stable homotopy and converges to unstable homotopy. (p. 1)
            %p …the Goodwillie tower is an inverse to stable homotopy in the same way as logarithm is an inverse to exponential. (p. 1)
            %p It is the point of this paper that the Goodwillie tower is the homotopy theoretic analog of logarithmic expansion, rather than of Taylor series. (p. 6)
          %p
            What’s going on, they say, is like finding a function of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^{x - 1}
            which best approximates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = e
          %p
            The functor from spaces to spaces which sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+loop+space"} infinite loop space
            underlying its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+spectrum"} suspension spectrum
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi colim
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^{\infty}\Sigma^{\infty} X = colim \Omega^n \Sigma^n X
          %p
            sends coproducts to products and is supposed to be like
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{x - 1}
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
          %p
            A homogeneous linear functor is defined to be one sending coproducts to products, so it is like an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+map"} exponential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
            is like a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithm"} logarithm
          %blockquote
            %p
              …our point of view is that stable homotopy is analogous to the function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{x - 1}
              rather than to a linear function, and the Goodwillie tower is an infinite product, rather than an infinite sum, namely it is analogous to the product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mn 2
                        %mn 2
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 3
                      %mn 3
                  %mi …
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi ln
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  e^{x - 1} \cdot e^{-\frac{(x - 1)^2}{2}} \cdot e^{\frac{(x - 1)^3}{3}} \ldots = e^{ln(1 + (x - 1))} = x.
          %blockquote
            %p (p. 2)
          %p
            Gregory Arone in an MO
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/32071/is-the-dual-notion-of-a-presheaf-useful"} answer
          %blockquote
            %p
              Covariant functors from the category of pointed sets to the category of pointed topological spaces are sometimes called
              = succeed "-spaces," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gamma-space"} spaces
              model infinite loop spaces (and therefore connective spectra) and are very helpful for understanding stable homotopy theory.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              \-spaces also serve as a model for particularly well-behaved covariant functors from the category of pointed topological spaces to itself. Of course, these functors play an important role in topology as well. I like to think of Goodwillie’s Calculus of Homotopy Functors (and also of Michael Weiss’s Orthogonal Calculus) as a kind of “sheaf theory for covariant functors”. In these theories, covariant functors are analogous to presheaves and linear functors are analogous to sheaves (The definition of a linear functor is essentially a homotopy-invariant version of the definition of a sheaf). The process of approximating a general functor by a linear one is analogous to sheafification, and so forth. These theories provide methods for studying certain types of functors, but of course they also tell you something about the category of spaces itself.
          %p Eric Finster’s research statement
          %blockquote
            %p One tantalizing aspect of the Goodwillie calculus is that it suggests the possibility of thinking geometrically about the global structure of homotopy theory. In this interpretation, the category of spectra plays the role of the tangent space to the category of spaces at the one-point space. Moreover, the identity functor from spaces to spaces is not linear…and one can interpret this as saying that spaces have some kind of non-trivial curvature.
          %p
            However, Goodwillie remarks in the
            %a{:href => "#OWR2004"} report
            (p. 905) on a Oberwolfach meeting.:
          %blockquote
            %p Rhetorical question: If the first derivative of the identity is the identity matrix, why is the second derivative not zero? Answer: Some of the terminology of homotopy calculus works better for functors from spaces to spectra than for functors from spaces to spaces. Specifically, since “linearity” means taking pushout squares to pullback squares, the identity functor is not linear and the composition of two linear functors is not linear.
            %p Attempted cryptic remark: Unlike the category of spectra, where pushouts are the same as pullbacks, the category of spaces may be thought of has having nonzero curvature.
            %p Correction: After the talk Boekstedt asked about that remark. We discussed the matter at length and found more than one connection on the category of spaces, but none that was not flat. In fact curvature is the wrong thing to look for. There are in some sense exactly two tangent connections on the category of spaces (or should we say on any model category?). Both are flat and torsion-free. There is a map between them, so it is meaningful to subtract them. As is well-known in differential geometry, the difference between two connections is a 1-form with values in endomorphisms (whereas the curvature is a 2-form with values in endomorphisms). Thus there is a way of discussing the discrepancy between pushouts and pullbacks in the language of differential geometry, but it is a tensor field of a different type from what I had guessed.
          %p
            This is from the
            %a{:href => "#OWR2004"} report
            (p. 905) on a Oberwolfach meeting. The table on p. 900 also makes comparisons to differential geometry.
          %p Chris Schommer-Pries
          %blockquote
            %p
              Any linear functor from spaces to spaces is a generalized cohomology theory. More precisely, there is a model category on the functors from spaces to spaces called the model category of W-spaces. Really I should be using pointed spaces here. This model category is one of the standard models for the category spectra and so the fibrant objects can be thought of as the (co)homology theories. The fibrant objects are precisely those functors which are linear in Goodwillie’s sense. The example
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S P^{\infty}
              corresponds to ordinary cohomology (well there is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
              issue, but let’s ignore that). In general evaluating the linear functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              on a space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              gives you a space which should be thought of as the smash product of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p So now why should spectra/cohomology theories be thought of as linear functors? Well if you think of spectra as analogous to abelian groups, then applying a spectrum to a space (i.e. smashing with it) is a linearization of that space.
            %p
              Following this analogy, if we now have any old functor from space to spaces we can take its fibrant replacement in
              = succeed "-spaces." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            See also Eric Finster’s blog post
            %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20121114044441/https://curiousreasoning.wordpress.com/2010/08/02/thoughts-on-the-goodwillie-calculus/"} Thoughts on the Goodwillie Calculus
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#toposes_of_polynomial_functors
